WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.540
Many reactions of alkynes are similar to those
of alkenes, so I recommend watching our alkene

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.050
video first.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:12.839
Let’s begin with a few alkyne-forming reactions.

00:00:12.839 --> 00:00:18.720
The first method we’ll examine is a multistep
process called the Corey–Fuchs reaction.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.670
An aldehyde is treated with a mixture of carbon
tetrabromide and triphenylphosphene.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:32.170
CBr4 reacts with triphenylphosphene to form
this organophosphorous compound, or Wittig

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:37.830
reagent, which reacts with the aldehyde to
form a dibromoalkene.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:45.030
The bromide is subsequently treated with the
strong base n-butyllithium to cause an elimination

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.620
reaction, forming the alkyne after treatment
with water.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.530
Dihalides, which may be formed by halogenation
of alkenes, can be treated with two equivalents

00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:01.220
of the strong base, sodium amide, to form
internal alkynes.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:07.320
This process can also be used to form terminal
alkynes; however, three equivalents of the

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.630
strong base are required.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.400
Let’s examine why by looking at the mechanism.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.070
The first molar equivalent of sodium amide
deprotonates the molecule and causes elimination

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:24.230
of bromine, forming an intermediate alkene.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:29.990
The second equivalent repeats this process,
creating the alkyne.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:35.550
Terminal alkynes have a pKa of about 24, which
makes them acidic enough to be deprotonated

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.000
by sodium amide.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.750
The third equivalent deprotonates the terminal
H atom, creating an anion, which is why water

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:50.960
is needed in both of the reactions that form
terminal alkynes.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.090
The fact that alkynes can be deprotonated
with strong base leads us to our first reaction

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.150
of alkynes.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:05.690
A deprotonated alkyne, or acetylide anion,
can be alkylated with a primary alkyl halide.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:10.750
It is important that the alkyl halide is primary.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:16.950
The acetylide ion is a strong base, so it
can cause elimination if reacted with hindered

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.610
alkyl halides.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:25.440
Alkynes can be reduced completely by hydrogenation,
forming alkanes.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.400
When a palladium catalyst treated with lead
salts, called Lindlar’s catalyst, is used

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:37.959
in alkyne hydrogenation, the alkyne may be
reduced to the Z-alkene.

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:44.480
The E- alkene can be formed by treating the
alkyne with sodium in liquid ammonia.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.730
This is a radical reaction; sodium transfers
a single electron to the alkyne, forming a

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.540
radical anion.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:00.880
This anion adopts the more stable conformation,
putting the methyl groups on opposite sides

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.459
of a double bond before it is protonated by
ammonia.

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:12.090
The mechanism here is abbreviated, but sodium
donates another electron, forming a new anion,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.480
which is again protonated by ammonia.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:21.530
Like alkenes, alkynes can add halogens across
multiple bonds.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:29.239
Bromination or chlorination gives the tetrahalide;
here, addition of Br2 across the bond occurs

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:33.150
via a bromonium ion.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:38.320
Halides can also be added using hydrobromic
or hydrochloric acid.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:44.140
Note the regioselectivity; two equivalents
of the halide are added to the more substituted

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.190
side of the alkyne.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:52.660
We see Markovnikov addition; the proton adds
to the less substituted carbon to form the

00:03:52.660 --> 00:04:00.170
more stable carbocation, and the addition
occurs again in the same way to the intermediate

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.190
chloroalkene.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:08.900
Terminal alkynes can be converted to aldehydes
or ketones selectively with appropriate reagent

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.959
choice.

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:16.950
Hydroboration/oxidation, a two-stage process
where the alkyne is first treated with a boron

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:22.190
reagent, then oxidized, produces an aldehyde.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:28.139
The boron reagent adds to the least hindered
side—the terminus of the alkyne.

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:34.509
The large boron reagent prevents attack on
the intermediate, and the oxidation step gives

00:04:34.509 --> 00:04:41.719
an enol, which tautomerizes to the more stable
ketone.

00:04:41.719 --> 00:04:47.210
Oxymercuration can be used to produce ketones
directly from alkynes.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:52.370
The alkyne reacts to form a mercurinium ion.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:58.380
Water then attacks at the more substituted
carbon atom, since the mercurinium ion has

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:05.520
carbocation resonance, and the secondary carbocation
contributes more to the overall structure

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.189
than the primary carbocation.

00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:16.469
Again, the enol tautomerizes to the ketone,
and sulfuric acid protonates the carbonyl.

00:05:16.469 --> 00:05:23.650
This promotes elimination of mercury forming
another enol, and a final tautomerization

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:24.989
gives the ketone.

